1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,800
Chapter 6: Placental Abnormalities.

3
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:22,500
Normal Placenta Shape And Size Variants Extrachorial Placentation Circulatory Disturbances Placental Calcification Placental Tumors Amniochorion Umbilical Cord References During pregnancy, the placenta provides the indispensable interface between mother and fetus .

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:35,275
However, in part due to inaccessibility throughout gestation, the placenta’s anatomy, physiology, and molecular structure still remain some of the most understudied and intriguing topics in obstetrics.

5
00:00:35,275 --> 00:00:48,425
Furthermore, the parallels between placental formation and cancer afford opportunities to understand tumor biology and pathogenesis (Costanzo, 2018; Guttmacher, 2014).

6
00:00:48,425 --> 00:00:56,612
Visual placental inspection by the obstetrician is recommended, but routine pathological examination is not mandatory.

7
00:00:56,612 --> 00:01:02,812
Indeed, specific conditions that merit submission for detailed inspection are still debated.

8
00:01:02,812 --> 00:01:17,287
For example, the College of American Pathologists recommends placental examination for an extensive list of indications, however many providers are not aware (Langston, 1997; Odibo, 2016).

9
00:01:17,287 --> 00:01:21,437
Moreover, data are insufficient to support all of these.

10
00:01:21,437 --> 00:01:26,300
At minimum, the placenta and cord should be inspected in the delivery room.

11
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:36,462
The decision to request pathological examination should be based on clinical and placental findings (Redline, 2008; Roberts, 2008).

12
00:01:36,462 --> 00:01:38,762
Table 6-1.

13
00:01:38,762 --> 00:01:44,112
Some Indications for Placental Pathological Examinationa Normal Placenta.

14
00:01:44,112 --> 00:01:55,100
At term, the typical placenta weighs 470 g, is round to oval with a 22-cm diameter, and has a central thickness of 2.5 cm .

15
00:01:55,100 --> 00:02:01,600
It is composed of a placental disc, extraplacental membranes, and three-vessel umbilical cord.

16
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:09,825
The disc surface that lies against the uterine wall is the basal plate, which is divided by clefts into portions—termed cotyledons.

17
00:02:09,825 --> 00:02:12,975
The fetal surface is the chorionic plate.

18
00:02:12,975 --> 00:02:17,325
Here, the umbilical cord inserts, typically in the center.

19
00:02:17,325 --> 00:02:26,500
Large fetal vessels that originate from the cord vessels then spread and branch across the chorionic plate before entering stem villi of the placenta parenchyma.

20
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:31,750
In tracing these, fetal arteries almost invariably cross over veins.

21
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:39,637
The chorionic plate and its vessels are covered by thin amnion, which can be easily peeled away from a postdelivery specimen.

22
00:02:39,637 --> 00:02:55,700
During prenatal sonographic examinations, multiple societies, including the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine (2018), recommend identifying and recording placental location and its relationship to the internal cervical os.

23
00:02:55,700 --> 00:03:06,237
As seen sonographically, the normal placenta is homogenous and 2 to 4 cm thick, lies against the myometrium, and indents into the amnionic sac.

24
00:03:06,237 --> 00:03:14,937
The retroplacental space is a hypoechoic area that separates the myometrium from the basal plate and measures less than 1 to 2 cm.

25
00:03:14,937 --> 00:03:22,312
The umbilical cord is also imaged, its fetal and placental insertion sites examined, and its vessels counted.

26
00:03:22,312 --> 00:03:32,475
Many placental lesions can be identified grossly or sonographically, but other abnormalities require histopathological examination for clarification.

27
00:03:32,475 --> 00:03:46,862
A detailed description of these is beyond the scope of this chapter, and interested readers are referred to textbooks by Benirschke (2012), Fox (2007), and Faye-Petersen (2006) and their colleagues.

28
00:03:46,862 --> 00:03:57,750
Moreover, the placenta accreta spectrum, placenta previa, and gestational trophoblastic disease are presented in detail in Chapters 41 and 20, respectively.

29
00:03:57,750 --> 00:04:00,237
Shape And Size Variants.

30
00:04:00,237 --> 00:04:06,550
Of variants, placentas may infrequently form as separate, nearly equally sized discs.

31
00:04:06,550 --> 00:04:12,537
This bilobate placenta may also be called bipartite placenta or placenta duplex.

32
00:04:12,537 --> 00:04:21,000
In these, the cord inserts between the two placental lobes— either into a connecting chorionic bridge or into intervening membranes.

33
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,100
A placenta containing three or more equivalently sized lobes is rare and termed multilobate.

34
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:37,700
Unlike this equal distribution, one or more disparately smaller accessory lobes—succenturiate lobes—may develop in the membranes at a distance from the main placenta .

35
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:41,387
These lobes have vessels that course through the membranes.

36
00:04:41,387 --> 00:04:45,887
Of clinical importance, if these vessels overlie the cervix

37
00:04:46,036 --> 00:04:53,073
to create a vasa previa, dangerous fetal hemorrhage can follow vessel laceration (p. 115).

38
00:04:53,023 --> 00:05:01,698
An accessory lobe can also be retained in the uterus after delivery to cause postpartum uterine atony and hemorrhage or later endometritis.

39
00:05:01,698 --> 00:05:03,973
Figure 6-1.

40
00:05:03,973 --> 00:05:06,136
Succenturiate lobe.

41
00:05:06,136 --> 00:05:13,386
A. Vessels extend from the main placental disc to supply the small round succenturiate lobe located to the left.

42
00:05:13,386 --> 00:05:17,123
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Rachel Gardner.

43
00:05:17,123 --> 00:05:24,336
B. Sonographic imaging with color Doppler shows the main placental disc implanted posteriorly (asterisk).

44
00:05:24,336 --> 00:05:30,436
The succenturiate lobe is located on the anterior uterine wall across the amnionic cavity.

45
00:05:30,436 --> 00:05:39,173
Vessels are identified as the long red and blue crossing tubular structures that travel within the membranes to connect these two portions of placenta.

46
00:05:39,173 --> 00:05:43,373
Rarely, the placental surface area varies from the norm.

47
00:05:43,373 --> 00:05:48,836
With placenta membranacea, villi cover all or nearly all the uterine cavity.

48
00:05:48,836 --> 00:05:55,386
This may occasionally give rise to serious hemorrhage because of associated placenta previa or accreta .

49
00:05:55,386 --> 00:05:59,811
A ring-shaped placenta may be a variant of placenta membranacea.

50
00:05:59,811 --> 00:06:05,836
This placenta is annular, and a partial or complete ring of placental tissue is present.

51
00:06:05,836 --> 00:06:18,586
These abnormalities appear to be associated with a greater likelihood of antepartum and postpartum bleeding and fetal-growth restriction (Faye-Petersen, 2006; Steemers, 1995).

52
00:06:18,586 --> 00:06:23,811
With placenta fenestrata, the central portion of a placental disc is missing.

53
00:06:23,811 --> 00:06:27,948
In some cases, there is an actual hole in the placenta.

54
00:06:27,948 --> 00:06:33,686
More often, only villous tissue is missing, and the chorionic plate remains intact.

55
00:06:33,686 --> 00:06:40,898
During pregnancy, the normal placenta increases its thickness at a rate that approximates 1 mm per week.

56
00:06:40,898 --> 00:06:49,023
Although not measured as a component of routine sonographic evaluation, this thickness typically does not exceed 40 mm .

57
00:06:49,023 --> 00:06:56,336
Placentomegaly defines those thicker than 40 mm and commonly results from striking villous enlargement.

58
00:06:56,336 --> 00:07:10,973
Underlying maternal etiologies are diabetes mellitus or severe anemia, whereas fetal sources include hydrops, anemia, syphilis, toxoplasmosis, or infection caused by parvovirus or cytomegalovirus.

59
00:07:10,973 --> 00:07:15,361
In these conditions, the placenta is homogeneously thickened.

60
00:07:15,361 --> 00:07:19,511
In other cases placentas are thick but inhomogeneous.

61
00:07:19,511 --> 00:07:22,411
Partial mole is a classic example.

62
00:07:22,411 --> 00:07:29,711
The thickened placenta contains edematous villi, which appear as multiple, small, anechoic placental cysts .

63
00:07:29,711 --> 00:07:34,486
Cystic vesicles also are seen with placental mesenchymal dysplasia.

64
00:07:34,486 --> 00:07:39,611
In this rare condition, vesicles correspond to enlarged stem villi.

65
00:07:39,611 --> 00:07:47,661
However, unlike molar pregnancy, trophoblast proliferation is not excessive, and chromosomal complements are diploid .

66
00:07:47,661 --> 00:07:57,836
Rather than villous enlargement, inhomogeneous placentomegaly often may result from collections of blood or fibrin, which impart heterogeneity to the placenta.

67
00:07:57,836 --> 00:08:10,123
Examples of this are discussed later and include massive perivillous fibrin deposition, intervillous or subchorionic thromboses, and large retroplacental hematomas (p. 110).

68
00:08:10,123 --> 00:08:12,748
Extrachorial Placentation.

69
00:08:12,748 --> 00:08:20,561
The chorionic plate normally extends to the periphery of the placental disc and has a diameter similar to that of the basal plate.

70
00:08:20,561 --> 00:08:30,611
With extrachorial placentation, however, the chorionic plate fails to extend to this periphery and leads to a chorionic plate that is smaller than the basal plate .

71
00:08:30,611 --> 00:08:34,923
Circummarginate and circumvallate placentas are the two types.

72
00:08:34,923 --> 00:08:43,048
In a circummarginate placenta, fibrin and old hemorrhage lie between the placental disc and the overlying sheer amniochorion.

73
00:08:43,048 --> 00:08:54,036
In contrast, with a circumvallate placenta, the chorionic plate periphery is a thickened, opaque, gray-white circular ridge composed of a double fold of chorion and amnion.

74
00:08:54,036 --> 00:08:56,286
Figure 6-2.

75
00:08:56,286 --> 00:09:04,986
A. In this illustration, circummarginate (left) and circumvallate (right) varieties of extrachorial placentation are shown.

76
00:09:04,986 --> 00:09:10,111
A circummarginate placenta is covered by a single layer of amniochorion.

77
00:09:10,111 --> 00:09:16,086
B. This transabdominal grayscale sonographic image shows a circumvallate placenta.

78
00:09:16,086 --> 00:09:22,898
The double fold of amnion and chorion creates a broad, opaque white ring and ridge on the fetal surface.

79
00:09:22,898 --> 00:09:27,973
Sonographically, the circumvallate fold can be seen as a thick, linear

80
00:09:28,132 --> 00:09:32,407
band of echoes extending from one placental edge to the other.

81
00:09:32,357 --> 00:09:41,107
On cross section, however, it appears as two “shelves,” with each lying above an opposing placental margin (see Fig. 6-2).

82
00:09:41,107 --> 00:09:45,494
This anatomy can help differentiate this shelf from other bands .

83
00:09:45,494 --> 00:09:47,782
Table 6-2.

84
00:09:47,782 --> 00:10:06,457
Sonographic Bands During Pregnancy In small observational studies of circumvallate placenta diagnosed postpartum, it was associated with increased risk for antepartum bleeding, abruption, fetal demise, and preterm birth (Suzuki, 2008; Taniguchi, 2014).

85
00:10:06,457 --> 00:10:17,657
In a prospective sonographic investigation of 17 cases, however, Shen and associates (2007a) found most circumvallate placentas to be transient.

86
00:10:17,657 --> 00:10:20,282
Persistent cases were benign.

87
00:10:20,282 --> 00:10:31,157
In general, most otherwise uncomplicated pregnancies with either type of extrachorial placentation have normal outcomes, and no increased surveillance is usually required.

88
00:10:31,157 --> 00:10:33,844
Circulatory Disturbances.

89
00:10:33,844 --> 00:10:47,219
Functionally, placental perfusion disorders can be grouped into: (1) those in which maternal blood flow to or within the intervillous space is disrupted, and (2) those with disturbed fetal blood flow through the villi.

90
00:10:47,219 --> 00:10:52,169
These lesions are frequently identified in the normal, mature placenta.

91
00:10:52,169 --> 00:10:59,719
Although they can limit maximal placental blood flow, functional reserve within the placental prevents harm in most cases.

92
00:10:59,719 --> 00:11:06,707
Indeed, some estimate that up to 30 percent of placental villi can be lost without untoward fetal effects .

93
00:11:06,707 --> 00:11:11,932
If extensive, however, these lesions can profoundly limit fetal growth.

94
00:11:11,932 --> 00:11:20,494
Lesions that disrupt perfusion are frequently seen grossly or sonographically, whereas smaller lesions are seen only histologically.

95
00:11:20,494 --> 00:11:32,619
With sonography, many of these, such as subchorionic fibrin deposition, perivillous fibrin deposition, and intervillous thrombosis, appear as focal sonolucencies within the placenta.

96
00:11:32,619 --> 00:11:44,782
Greater magnetic resonance (MR) imaging use in pregnancy has permitted detection and further characterization of these lesions (Bockoven, 2020; Capuani, 2017).

97
00:11:44,782 --> 00:11:54,107
Importantly, in the absence of maternal or fetal complications, small isolated placental sonolucencies are considered incidental findings.

98
00:11:54,107 --> 00:11:57,294
# Maternal Blood Flow Disruption.

99
00:11:57,294 --> 00:12:04,857
Subchorionic Fibrin Deposition These collections are caused by slowing of maternal blood flow within the intervillous space.

100
00:12:04,857 --> 00:12:12,832
In the upper portion of this space near the chorionic plate, blood stasis is prominent and leads to subsequent fibrin deposition.

101
00:12:12,832 --> 00:12:22,832
In viewing the placental fetal surface, subchorionic lesions are seen as white or yellow, firm, round, elevated plaques just beneath the chorionic plate.

102
00:12:22,832 --> 00:12:35,582
Perivillous Fibrin Deposition Stasis of maternal blood flow around an individual villus also results in fibrin deposition and can lead to diminished villous oxygenation and necrosis of syncytiotrophoblast .

103
00:12:35,582 --> 00:12:42,182
These small yellow-white placental nodules are grossly visible within the parenchyma of a sectioned placenta.

104
00:12:42,182 --> 00:12:46,207
Within limits, these reflect normal placental aging.

105
00:12:46,207 --> 00:12:59,057
Deposition that affects >25 percent of villi is associated with fetal-growth restriction and adverse neonatal outcomes (Devisme, 2017; Spinillo, 2019).

106
00:12:59,057 --> 00:13:01,332
Figure 6-3.

107
00:13:01,332 --> 00:13:07,132
Potential sites of maternally and fetally related placental circulatory disturbances.

108
00:13:07,132 --> 00:13:09,544
Maternal Floor Infarction.

109
00:13:09,544 --> 00:13:18,882
This extreme variant of perivillous fibrin deposition is a dense fibrinoid layer within the placental basal plate and is erroneously termed an infarction.

110
00:13:18,882 --> 00:13:28,319
Maternal floor infarction has a thick, yellow or white, firm corrugated surface that impedes normal maternal blood flow into the intervillous space.

111
00:13:28,319 --> 00:13:38,782
In specific cases that extend up and beyond the basal plate to entrap villi and obliterate the intervillous space, the term massive perivillous fibrin deposition is used.

112
00:13:38,782 --> 00:13:49,894
The etiopathogenesis is unclear, but maternal auto- or alloimmunity appears contributory (FayePeterson, 2018; Romero, 2013).

113
00:13:49,894 --> 00:14:01,344
Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome and angiogenic factors involved with preeclampsia also have been implicated (Sebire, 2002; Whitten, 2013).

114
00:14:01,344 --> 00:14:08,119
These lesions are not reliably imaged with prenatal sonography, but they may create a thicker basal plate.

115
00:14:08,119 --> 00:14:11,394
Affected pregnancies are associated with

116
00:14:11,548 --> 00:14:20,910
miscarriage, fetal-growth restriction, preterm delivery, and stillbirth (Andres, 1990; Mandsager, 1994).

117
00:14:20,860 --> 00:14:26,010
Importantly, these adverse outcomes can recur in subsequent pregnancies.

118
00:14:26,010 --> 00:14:35,248
Intervillous Thrombus This is a collection of coagulated maternal blood normally found in the intervillous space mixed with fetal blood from a break in a villus.

119
00:14:35,248 --> 00:14:40,960
Grossly, these round or oval collections vary in size up to several centimeters.

120
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:46,873
They appear red if recent or white-yellow if older, and they develop at any placental depth.

121
00:14:46,873 --> 00:14:52,635
Intervillous thrombi are common and typically not associated with adverse fetal sequelae.

122
00:14:52,635 --> 00:15:07,485
These reflect potential communication between maternal and fetal circulations, and thus large lesions are one cause of elevated maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein (MSAFP) levels (Table 17-5, p. 338).

123
00:15:07,485 --> 00:15:16,698
Infarction Chorionic villi themselves receive oxygen solely from maternal circulation and specifically from blood supplied into the intervillous space.

124
00:15:16,698 --> 00:15:24,185
Any uteroplacental disease that diminishes or obstructs this supply can result in infarction of an individual villus.

125
00:15:24,185 --> 00:15:29,448
These are common lesions in mature placentas and are benign in limited numbers.

126
00:15:29,448 --> 00:15:34,085
If numerous, however, placental insufficiency can develop.

127
00:15:34,085 --> 00:15:42,823
When they are thick, centrally located, and randomly distributed, they may be associated with preeclampsia or lupus anticoagulant.

128
00:15:42,823 --> 00:15:48,623
Hematoma The maternal–placental–fetal unit can develop several hematoma types.

129
00:15:48,623 --> 00:16:19,723
As depicted in Figure 6-3, these include: (1) retroplacental hematoma—formed between the placenta and its adjacent decidua; (2) marginal hematoma— formed between the chorion and decidua at the placental periphery—known clinically as subchorionic hemorrhage; (3) subamnionic hematoma— derived of fetal vessel origin and found beneath the amnion but above the chorionic plate, and (4) subchorial thrombus along the roof of the intervillous space and beneath the chorionic plate.

130
00:16:19,723 --> 00:16:25,798
With this last type, massive subchorionic hematomas are also known as Breus moles.

131
00:16:25,798 --> 00:16:38,198
Sonographically, hematomas evolve with time and appear hyperechoic to isoechoic in the first week after hemorrhage, hypoechoic at 1 to 2 weeks, and finally, anechoic after 2 weeks.

132
00:16:38,198 --> 00:16:45,160
Most subchorionic hematomas visible sonographically are fairly small and of no clinical consequence .

133
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:56,623
However, extensive retroplacental, marginal, and subchorial collections are associated with higher rates of miscarriage, stillbirth, placental abruption, and preterm delivery .

134
00:16:56,623 --> 00:17:03,185
In essence, placental abruption is a large, clinically significant retroplacental hematoma.

135
00:17:03,185 --> 00:17:06,273
# Fetal Blood Flow Disruption.

136
00:17:06,273 --> 00:17:15,048
Fetal Vascular Malperfusion Placental lesions that arise from fetal circulatory disturbances are also depicted in Figure 6-3.

137
00:17:15,048 --> 00:17:22,685
Normally, deoxygenated fetal blood flows from the two umbilical arteries into arteries within the chorionic plate.

138
00:17:22,685 --> 00:17:28,060
These surface arteries divide and send branches out across the placental surface.

139
00:17:28,060 --> 00:17:31,660
These eventually supply individual stem villi.

140
00:17:31,660 --> 00:17:40,423
Remember that fetal blood is oxygenated within each villus by passive diffusion of oxygen from maternal blood contained within the intervillous space.

141
00:17:40,423 --> 00:17:47,323
Thus, with fetal vessel thrombosis, portions of the villus distal to the obstruction become nonfunctional.

142
00:17:47,323 --> 00:17:52,248
Normally, thrombi in limited numbers are found in mature placentas.

143
00:17:52,248 --> 00:18:08,623
If many villi are affected, which can be seen with preeclampsia, the fetus may suffer growth restriction, stillbirth, or nonreassuring fetal heart rate patterns (Chisholm, 2015; Lepais, 2014; Saleemuddin, 2010).

144
00:18:08,623 --> 00:18:14,398
Villous Vascular Lesions Villous capillaries show a spectrum of histological lesions.

145
00:18:14,398 --> 00:18:19,898
Chorangiosis describes an increased number of capillaries within terminal villi.

146
00:18:19,898 --> 00:18:31,348
Its definition requires ≥10 capillaries to be present in ≥10 villi in ≥10 fields viewed through a 10× lens .

147
00:18:31,348 --> 00:18:36,810
Clinically, long-standing hypoperfusion or hypoxia is thought to be causative .

148
00:18:36,810 --> 00:18:44,360
Focal chorangiosis is increased capillary vascularity in a significant portion of the placenta but not diffusely.

149
00:18:44,360 --> 00:18:51,573
In one small study, lower Apgar scores and fetal vascular malperfusion were associated outcomes .

150
00:18:51,573 --> 00:18:53,848
Prenatal detection of

151
00:18:54,004 --> 00:18:56,741
chorangiosis has been reported .

152
00:18:56,691 --> 00:19:03,704
Chorangiomatosis describes increased capillary number in stem villi, but terminal villi are spared.

153
00:19:03,704 --> 00:19:08,366
This finding has been linked with fetal-growth restriction and anomalies .

154
00:19:08,366 --> 00:19:15,079
Despite these associations, the clinical significance of both vascular conditions remains unclear.

155
00:19:15,079 --> 00:19:18,554
Chorioangiomas are described subsequently.

156
00:19:18,554 --> 00:19:25,641
Subamnionic Hematoma As noted earlier, these hematomas lie between the chorionic plate and amnion.

157
00:19:25,641 --> 00:19:35,254
They most often are acute iatrogenic events of no clinical consequence during third-stage labor when cord traction ruptures a vessel near the cord insertion.

158
00:19:35,254 --> 00:19:41,729
Large, chronic antepartum lesions may cause fetomaternal hemorrhage or fetal-growth restriction .

159
00:19:41,729 --> 00:19:46,929
They also may be confused with other placental masses such as chorioangioma.

160
00:19:46,929 --> 00:19:55,429
In most cases, color Doppler interrogation will show absent internal blood flow within a hematoma and permit differentiation .

161
00:19:55,429 --> 00:19:57,866
Placental Calcification.

162
00:19:57,866 --> 00:20:03,741
Calcium salts can be deposited throughout the placenta but are most common on the basal plate.

163
00:20:03,741 --> 00:20:12,491
Calcification accrues with advancing gestation, and greater degrees are associated with smoking and higher maternal serum calcium levels .

164
00:20:12,491 --> 00:20:23,116
These hyperechoic deposits can easily be seen sonographically, and a grading scale from 0 to 3 reflects increasing calcification with increasing numerical grade .

165
00:20:23,116 --> 00:20:31,979
Following this scheme, a grade 0 placenta is homogeneous, lacks calcification, and displays a smooth, flat chorionic plate.

166
00:20:31,979 --> 00:20:38,166
A grade 1 placenta has scattered echogenicities and subtle chorionic plate undulations.

167
00:20:38,166 --> 00:20:42,204
Grade 2 shows echogenic stippling at the basal plate.

168
00:20:42,204 --> 00:20:49,829
Large, echogenic comma shapes originate from an indented chorionic plate, but their curve falls short of the basal plate.

169
00:20:49,829 --> 00:21:00,454
Last, a grade 3 placenta has echogenic indentations extending from the chorionic plate to the basal plate, which create discrete components that resemble cotyledons.

170
00:21:00,454 --> 00:21:03,654
Basal plate densities also increase.

171
00:21:03,654 --> 00:21:12,066
This grading scale poorly predicts neonatal outcome near term (McKenna, 2005; Mirza, 2018).

172
00:21:12,066 --> 00:21:26,141
However, data from two small studies link grade 3 placenta prior to 32 weeks with stillbirth and some other adverse pregnancy outcomes (Chen, 2011, 2015; Mirza, 2018).

173
00:21:26,141 --> 00:21:28,166
Placental Tumors.

174
00:21:28,166 --> 00:21:30,754
# Chorioangioma.

175
00:21:30,754 --> 00:21:36,979
These benign tumors have components similar to the blood vessels and stroma of the chorionic villus.

176
00:21:36,979 --> 00:21:43,554
Also called chorangiomas, these placental tumors have an incidence that approximates 1 percent .

177
00:21:43,554 --> 00:21:50,579
In some cases, fetal-to-maternal hemorrhage across tumor capillaries leads to elevated levels of MSAFP.

178
00:21:50,579 --> 00:21:58,366
This typically prompts sonographic evaluation to exclude a neural-tube defect, which also shows high MSAFP levels.

179
00:21:58,366 --> 00:22:09,654
Sonographically, chorangiomas appear as a well-circumscribed, rounded, predominantly hypoechoic lesion lying near the chorionic plate and protruding into the amnionic cavity .

180
00:22:09,654 --> 00:22:22,766
Documenting increased blood flow by color Doppler helps to distinguish these lesions from other placental masses such as hematoma, partial hydatidiform mole, teratoma, metastases, and leiomyoma .

181
00:22:22,766 --> 00:22:28,504
Although rare, chorangiocarcinoma tumors mirror chorioangiomas clinically .

182
00:22:28,504 --> 00:22:30,841
Figure 6-4.

183
00:22:30,841 --> 00:22:33,429
Placental chorioangioma.

184
00:22:33,429 --> 00:22:40,854
A. Color Doppler imaging displays blood flow through a large chorioangioma with its border outlined by white arrows.

185
00:22:40,854 --> 00:22:47,966
B. Grossly, the chorioangioma is a round, wellcircumcised mass protruding from the fetal surface.

186
00:22:47,966 --> 00:22:51,991
Small chorioangiomas are usually asymptomatic.

187
00:22:51,991 --> 00:23:04,479
Large tumors, typically those measuring >4 cm, can create significant arteriovenous shunting within the placenta to cause high-output heart failure, hydrops, and fetal death .

188
00:23:04,479 --> 00:23:12,304
Compression or shearing of fetal erythrocytes within tumor vessels can lead to hemolysis and microangiopathic anemia .

189
00:23:12,304 --> 00:23:18,016
Hydramnios, preterm delivery, and fetal-growth restriction are other sequelae .

190
00:23:18,016 --> 00:23:25,591
Large tumor size and fetal hydrops are the primary determinants and signal a potential adverse perinatal outcome .

191
00:23:25,591 --> 00:23:33,454
Grayscale and color Doppler interrogation of the placenta and amnionic fluid volume are used to identify these tumors.

192
00:23:33,454 --> 00:23:39,729
Diagnostic tools that can affirm associated fetomaternal hemorrhage include MSAFP level and

193
00:23:39,892 --> 00:23:42,054
Kleihauer-Betke stain .

194
00:23:42,004 --> 00:23:52,017
With fetal concern, echocardiography assesses cardiac function, whereas middle cerebral artery interrogation is used to identify fetal anemia.

195
00:23:52,017 --> 00:23:58,579
Several fetal therapies interfere with the vascular supply to the tumor and reverse fetal heart failure.

196
00:23:58,579 --> 00:24:08,992
At specialized perinatal centers, endoscopic laser ablation of feeder vessels to the tumor is most frequently used and is associated with favorable fetal outcomes .

197
00:24:08,992 --> 00:24:20,629
Discussed in Chapter 16, fetal transfusion can treat serious anemia, amnioreduction can temporize hydramnios, and digoxin therapy can assist fetal heart failure.

198
00:24:20,629 --> 00:24:23,429
# Metastatic Tumors.

199
00:24:23,429 --> 00:24:27,967
Maternal malignant tumors rarely metastasize to the placenta.

200
00:24:27,967 --> 00:24:34,592
Of those that do, melanomas, leukemias and lymphomas, and breast cancer are the most common .

201
00:24:34,592 --> 00:24:38,842
Tumor cells usually are confined within the intervillous space.

202
00:24:38,842 --> 00:24:45,092
As a result, metastasis to the fetus is uncommon but is most often seen with melanoma .

203
00:24:45,092 --> 00:24:50,992
Similarly, cases in which fetal malignancy metastasizes to the placenta are rare .

204
00:24:50,992 --> 00:25:00,404
These are predominantly fetal neuroectodermal tumors, and only one case in the literature describes transplantation of tumor to the maternal uterus .

205
00:25:00,404 --> 00:25:02,442
Amniochorion.

206
00:25:02,442 --> 00:25:05,229
# Chorioamnionitis.

207
00:25:05,229 --> 00:25:12,729
Normal genital-tract flora can colonize and infect the membranes, umbilical cord, and eventually the fetus.

208
00:25:12,729 --> 00:25:19,192
Bacteria most commonly ascend after prolonged membrane rupture and during labor to cause infection.

209
00:25:19,192 --> 00:25:25,917
Organisms initially infect the chorion and adjacent decidua in the area overlying the internal os.

210
00:25:25,917 --> 00:25:32,654
Subsequently, progression leads to full-thickness involvement of the membranes—chorioamnionitis.

211
00:25:32,654 --> 00:25:40,067
Organisms often then spread along the chorioamnionic surface to colonize and replicate in amnionic fluid.

212
00:25:40,067 --> 00:25:50,067
Inflammation of the chorionic plate and of the umbilical cord—funisitis— may follow (Kim, 2015; Redline, 2012).

213
00:25:50,067 --> 00:25:57,754
Most commonly, chorioamnionitis is microscopic or occult and caused by a wide variety of microorganisms.

214
00:25:57,754 --> 00:26:07,092
This is frequently cited as a possible explanation for many otherwise unexplained cases of ruptured membranes, preterm labor, or both .

215
00:26:07,092 --> 00:26:17,017
In some cases, gross infection is characterized by visible membrane clouding and is sometimes accompanied by a foul odor that depends on bacterial species.

216
00:26:17,017 --> 00:26:20,254
# Other Membrane Abnormalities.

217
00:26:20,254 --> 00:26:29,217
Amnion nodosum is a condition characterized by numerous small, light-tan nodules affixed to the amnion that overlies the chorionic plate.

218
00:26:29,217 --> 00:26:38,204
These may be scraped off the fetal surface and contain deposits of fetal squames and fibrin that reflect prolonged and severe oligohydramnios .

219
00:26:38,204 --> 00:26:42,267
Two notable bands can be formed by the fetal membranes.

220
00:26:42,267 --> 00:26:51,579
First, amnionic band sequence is an anatomical disruption sequence in which amnion bands tether, constrict, or amputate fetal parts.

221
00:26:51,579 --> 00:26:56,567
Bands may form spontaneously or follow fetal surgery procedures .

222
00:26:56,567 --> 00:27:07,629
Amnionic bands commonly cause limb-reduction defects, facial clefts, or encephalocele (Barzilay, 2015; Guzmán-Huerta, 2013).

223
00:27:07,629 --> 00:27:11,267
Umbilical cord compromise is another sequela .

224
00:27:11,267 --> 00:27:21,904
Severe defects of the spine or ventral wall that accompany amnionic bands suggest a limb-body wall complex, described in Chapter 15 (p. 297).

225
00:27:21,904 --> 00:27:28,092
Sonography often first identifies the sequelae of this sequence rather than the bands themselves.

226
00:27:28,092 --> 00:27:32,842
As with any fetal anomaly, targeted sonography is indicated.

227
00:27:32,842 --> 00:27:45,329
Identification of a limb-reduction defect, an encephalocele in an atypical location, or an extremity with edema or positional deformity should prompt careful evaluation for amnionic bands.

228
00:27:45,329 --> 00:27:49,304
Management depends on the degree of anatomic deformity .

229
00:27:49,304 --> 00:27:59,842
Fetoscopic laser interruption of the band may be suitable in highly selected antepartum cases (Gueneuc, 2019; Javadian, 2013).

230
00:27:59,842 --> 00:28:08,029
Second, an amnionic sheet in contrast is formed by normal amniochorion draped over a preexisting uterine synechia.

231
00:28:08,029 --> 00:28:20,854
Generally, these sheets pose little fetal risk, although slightly higher rates of preterm membrane rupture and placental abruption have been described (Nelson, 2010; Tuuli, 2012).

232
00:28:20,854 --> 00:28:22,817
Umbilical Cord.

233
00:28:22,817 --> 00:28:24,792
# Length.

234
00:28:24,792 --> 00:28:36,979
Most umbilical cords at delivery measure 40 to 70 cm long, and very few measure <30 cm or >100 cm. Cord length is influenced

235
00:28:37,132 --> 00:28:41,469
positively by both maternal parity and body mass index .

236
00:28:41,419 --> 00:28:54,819
In retrospective studies, short cords have been linked with congenital malformations and intrapartum distress (Krakowiak, 2004; Linde, 2018; Yamamoto, 2016).

237
00:28:54,819 --> 00:29:04,957
Excessively long cords are linked with cord entanglement or prolapse and with fetal anomalies (Olaya-C, 2015; Rayburn, 1981).

238
00:29:04,957 --> 00:29:14,544
Because antenatal determination of cord length is technically limited, cord diameter has been evaluated as a predictive marker for fetal outcomes.

239
00:29:14,544 --> 00:29:20,569
Some have linked lean cords with poor fetal growth and large-diameter cords with macrosomia .

240
00:29:20,569 --> 00:29:29,007
However, the clinical utility of this parameter is still unclear (Cromi, 2007; Raio, 2003).

241
00:29:29,007 --> 00:29:31,082
# Coiling.

242
00:29:31,082 --> 00:29:36,982
Cord coiling characteristics are not currently part of standard sonographic evaluation.

243
00:29:36,982 --> 00:29:47,482
Usually the umbilical vessels spiral through the cord in a sinistral, that is, left-twisting direction (Fletcher, 1993; Lacro, 1987).

244
00:29:47,482 --> 00:29:54,444
The number of complete coils per centimeter of cord length is termed the umbilical coiling index—UCI .

245
00:29:54,444 --> 00:30:05,857
A normal, antepartum, sonographically derived UCI is 0.4, and this contrasts with a normal, postpartum, physically measured value of 0.2 .

246
00:30:05,857 --> 00:30:12,982
UCIs <10th percentile are considered hypocoiled, and those >90th percentile are hypercoiled.

247
00:30:12,982 --> 00:30:17,557
Clinically, the significance of coiling extremes is controversial.

248
00:30:17,557 --> 00:30:30,194
Some studies evaluating large, unselected cohorts find no associations between UCI values and poor neonatal outcome (Jessop, 2014; Pathak, 2010).

249
00:30:30,194 --> 00:30:47,669
In others, extremes are linked with various adverse outcomes but most consistently with intrapartum fetal heart rate abnormalities, preterm labor, or fetal-growth restriction (Chitra, 2012; de Laat, 2006; Pergialiotis, 2019).

250
00:30:47,669 --> 00:30:49,932
# Vessel Number.

251
00:30:49,932 --> 00:30:59,182
Counting cord vessel number is a standard component of anatomical evaluation during fetal sonographic examination and immediately after delivery .

252
00:30:59,182 --> 00:31:02,857
Embryos initially have two umbilical veins.

253
00:31:02,857 --> 00:31:11,644
In the first trimester, the right vein typically atrophies to leave one large vein to accompany the two, thick-walled umbilical arteries.

254
00:31:11,644 --> 00:31:16,744
Four-vessel cords are rare and often associated with congenital anomalies .

255
00:31:16,744 --> 00:31:21,607
If it is an isolated finding, however, prognosis can be good .

256
00:31:21,607 --> 00:31:23,994
Figure 6-5.

257
00:31:23,994 --> 00:31:29,919
Two umbilical arteries are typically documented sonographically in the second trimester.

258
00:31:29,919 --> 00:31:36,069
They encircle the fetal bladder (asterisk) as extensions of the superior vesical arteries.

259
00:31:36,069 --> 00:31:47,282
A. In this color Doppler sonographic image, a single umbilical artery, shown in red, runs along the bladder wall before joining the umbilical vein (blue) in the cord.

260
00:31:47,282 --> 00:31:53,832
Remember with color Doppler that color signifies only blood flow direction relative to the transducer.

261
00:31:53,832 --> 00:31:58,944
B. A cross section of a floating cord segment shows the two vessels of the cord.

262
00:31:58,944 --> 00:32:05,032
The smaller circle is the single umbilical artery and the larger circle, the umbilical vein.

263
00:32:05,032 --> 00:32:10,469
The most common aberration is that of a single umbilical artery (SUA).

264
00:32:10,469 --> 00:32:20,344
Its cited incidence is 0.63 percent in liveborn neonates, 1.92 percent in perinatal deaths, and 3 percent in twins .

265
00:32:20,344 --> 00:32:24,882
Fetuses with major malformations frequently have an SUA.

266
00:32:24,882 --> 00:32:32,832
Thus, its identification often prompts consideration for targeted sonography and possibly fetal echocardiography.

267
00:32:32,832 --> 00:32:41,532
The most frequent anomalies are cardiovascular and genitourinary (Hua, 2010; Murphy-Kaulbeck, 2010).

268
00:32:41,532 --> 00:32:50,857
In an anomalous fetus, an SUA greatly increases the aneuploidy risk, and amniocentesis is recommended for karyotype assessment .

269
00:32:50,857 --> 00:33:01,882
If targeted sonography finds otherwise normal anatomy, an isolated SUA in an otherwise low-risk pregnancy does not significantly raise the fetal aneuploidy risk.

270
00:33:01,882 --> 00:33:16,319
However, as an isolated finding, it has been associated with fetal-growth restriction and perinatal death in some but not all studies (Chetty-John, 2010; Ebbing, 2019; Voskamp, 2013).

271
00:33:16,319 --> 00:33:23,194
Thus, clinical monitoring of growth is reasonable, but the value of sonographic surveillance is unclear.

272
00:33:23,194 --> 00:33:28,194
In contrast, a fused umbilical artery with a shared lumen is rare.

273
00:33:28,194 --> 00:33:33,807
It arises from failure of the two arteries to split during embryological development.

274
00:33:33,807 --> 00:33:40,032
The common lumen may extend through the entire cord, but, if partial, it is typically found

275
00:33:40,180 --> 00:33:42,967
near the placental insertion site .

276
00:33:42,917 --> 00:33:52,567
In one report, these malformations were associated with a higher incidence of marginal or velamentous cord insertion but not of congenital fetal anomalies .

277
00:33:52,567 --> 00:34:02,830
Found in most placentas, the Hyrtl anastomosis is a connection between the two umbilical arteries, and it lies near the cord’s insertion into the placenta.

278
00:34:02,830 --> 00:34:08,492
This anastomosis acts physiologically to equalize pressures between the arteries .

279
00:34:08,492 --> 00:34:19,430
The resulting redistribution of pressure gradients and blood flow improves placental perfusion, especially during uterine contractions or during compression of one umbilical artery.

280
00:34:19,430 --> 00:34:26,167
Fetuses with an SUA lack this safety valve (Raio, 1999, 2001).

281
00:34:26,167 --> 00:34:28,892
# Remnants and Cysts.

282
00:34:28,892 --> 00:34:37,992
Several structures are housed in the umbilical cord during fetal development, and their remnants may be seen when the mature cord is inspected transversely.

283
00:34:37,992 --> 00:34:51,755
Indeed, in grossly sectioned cords, remnants of the allantoic duct, vitelline duct, and embryonic vessels are found in 25 to 50 percent (Grottling, 2019; Jauniaux, 1989).

284
00:34:51,755 --> 00:34:57,455
These are not associated with congenital malformations or perinatal complications.

285
00:34:57,455 --> 00:35:01,305
Cysts occasionally are found along the course of the cord.

286
00:35:01,305 --> 00:35:04,505
They are designated according to their origin.

287
00:35:04,505 --> 00:35:12,955
True cysts are epithelium-lined remnants of the allantoic or vitelline ducts and tend to be located closer to the fetal insertion site.

288
00:35:12,955 --> 00:35:20,842
In contrast, the more common pseudocysts form from local degeneration of Wharton jelly and occur anywhere along the cord.

289
00:35:20,842 --> 00:35:24,155
Both have a similar sonographic appearance.

290
00:35:24,155 --> 00:35:38,167
Single umbilical cord cysts identified in the first trimester tend to resolve completely, however, multiple cysts may portend miscarriage or aneuploidy (Ghezzi, 2003; Hannaford, 2013).

291
00:35:38,167 --> 00:35:49,080
Cysts persisting beyond this time are associated with a risk for structural defects and chromosomal anomalies (Bonilla, 2010; Zangen, 2010).

292
00:35:49,080 --> 00:35:51,255
# Insertion.

293
00:35:51,255 --> 00:35:59,592
The cord normally inserts centrally into the placental disc, but eccentric, marginal, or velamentous insertions are variants.

294
00:35:59,592 --> 00:36:05,117
Of these, eccentric insertions in general pose no identifiable fetal risk.

295
00:36:05,117 --> 00:36:13,780
Marginal insertion is a common variant—sometimes referred to as a battledore placenta—in which the cord anchors at the placental margin.

296
00:36:13,780 --> 00:36:21,317
In one population-based study, the rate was 6 percent in singleton gestations and 11 percent in twins .

297
00:36:21,317 --> 00:36:35,230
This common insertion variant rarely causes problems, but it and velamentous insertion occasionally result in the cord being pulled off during delivery of the placenta (Ebbing, 2015; Luo, 2013).

298
00:36:35,230 --> 00:36:41,192
In monochorionic twins, marginal insertion may be associated with weight discordance .

299
00:36:41,192 --> 00:36:49,192
With velamentous insertion, the umbilical vessels characteristically travel within the membranes before reaching the placental margin .

300
00:36:49,192 --> 00:36:55,192
The incidence of velamentous insertion approximates 1 percent but is 6 percent with twins .

301
00:36:55,192 --> 00:37:02,630
It is more commonly seen with placenta previa (Papinniemi, 2007; Räisänen, 2012).

302
00:37:02,630 --> 00:37:11,667
Antenatal diagnosis is possible sonographically, and cord vessels are seen traveling along the uterine wall before entering the placental disc.

303
00:37:11,667 --> 00:37:18,442
Clinically, vessels are vulnerable to compression, which may lead to fetal hypoperfusion and acidemia.

304
00:37:18,442 --> 00:37:35,105
Higher associated rates of low Apgar scores, stillbirth, preterm delivery, and small for gestational age have been noted (de Los Reyes, 2018; Ebbing, 2017; Esakoff, 2015; Vahanian, 2015).

305
00:37:35,105 --> 00:37:41,117
Accordingly, monitoring of fetal growth is reasonable either clinically or sonographically .

306
00:37:41,117 --> 00:37:43,530
Figure 6-6.

307
00:37:43,530 --> 00:37:46,080
Velamentous cord insertion.

308
00:37:46,080 --> 00:37:50,655
A. The umbilical cord inserts into the membranes (arrow).

309
00:37:50,655 --> 00:37:57,330
From here, the cord vessels branch and are supported only by membrane until they reach the placental disc.

310
00:37:57,330 --> 00:38:08,517
B. When viewed sonographically and using color Doppler, the cord vessels appear to lie against the myometrium as they travel to insert into the margin of the placental disc (P).

311
00:38:08,517 --> 00:38:16,217
Last, with the rare furcate insertion, umbilical vessels lose their protective Wharton jelly shortly before they insert.

312
00:38:16,217 --> 00:38:23,467
As a result, they are covered only by an amnion sheath and prone to compression, twisting, and thrombosis.

313
00:38:23,467 --> 00:38:27,617
Vasa Previa With this condition, vessels travel within

314
00:38:27,772 --> 00:38:31,334
the membranes and overlie the cervical os.

315
00:38:31,284 --> 00:38:39,597
There, they can be torn with cervical dilation or membrane rupture, and laceration can lead to rapid fetal exsanguination.

316
00:38:39,597 --> 00:38:44,922
Over the cervix, vessels can also be compressed by a presenting fetal part .

317
00:38:44,922 --> 00:38:58,384
Vasa previa may be more common than previously estimated, and rates are 1 case in 338 to 365 pregnancies (Hasegawa, 2012; Klahr, 2019).

318
00:38:58,384 --> 00:39:11,272
Vasa previa is classified as type 1, in which vessels are part of a velamentous cord insertion, and type 2, in which involved vessels span between portions of a bilobate or a succenturiate placenta .

319
00:39:11,272 --> 00:39:23,309
Two other risks are conception with in vitro fertilization and secondtrimester placenta previa, with or without later migration (Baulies, 2007; Schachter, 2003).

320
00:39:23,309 --> 00:39:39,159
Compared with intrapartum diagnosis, antepartum diagnosis greatly improves the perinatal survival rate, which ranges from 97 to 100 percent (Oyelese, 2004; Swank, 2016; Zhang, 2021).

321
00:39:39,159 --> 00:39:45,309
Thus, vasa previa is ideally identified early, although this is not always possible.

322
00:39:45,309 --> 00:39:51,972
Effective screening for vasa previa begins during scheduled midtrimester sonographic examination.

323
00:39:51,972 --> 00:40:02,072
In suspicious cases, transvaginal sonography is added and shows cord vessels inserting into the membranes and vessels running above the cervical internal os .

324
00:40:02,072 --> 00:40:12,647
Routine color Doppler interrogation of the placental cord insertion site, particularly in cases of placenta previa or low-lying placenta, may aid its detection.

325
00:40:12,647 --> 00:40:16,959
With this, the vessel waveform reflects the fetal heart rate.

326
00:40:16,959 --> 00:40:22,797
In one systematic review, the median prenatal detection rate was 93 percent .

327
00:40:22,797 --> 00:40:25,134
Figure 6-7.

328
00:40:25,134 --> 00:40:27,034
Vasa previa.

329
00:40:27,034 --> 00:40:36,222
A. Using color Doppler, an umbilical vessel (red linear structure) is seen overlying the internal os and cervical canal (arrows).

330
00:40:36,222 --> 00:40:43,159
At the bottom, the Doppler waveform seen with this vasa previa has the typical appearance of an umbilical artery.

331
00:40:43,159 --> 00:40:58,309
B. The amniotomy site (held open by hemostat), which was created at the time of cesarean hysterotomy, illustrates how fetal vessels may be lacerated and why fetal delivery should be prompt (Reproduced with permission from Dr. Julie Lo.

332
00:40:58,309 --> 00:41:10,697
Once vasa previa is identified, subsequent imaging is reasonable because up to 39 percent of cases ultimately resolve (Erfani, 2019; Klahr, 2019).

333
00:41:10,697 --> 00:41:14,297
Bed rest apparently has no added advantage.

334
00:41:14,297 --> 00:41:24,884
Antenatal corticosteroids can be provided as indicated or given prophylactically at 28 to 32 weeks’ gestation to cover possible urgent preterm delivery.

335
00:41:24,884 --> 00:41:35,322
Antenatal hospitalization may be considered at 30 to 34 weeks to permit surveillance and expedited delivery for labor, bleeding, or rupture of membranes.

336
00:41:35,322 --> 00:41:42,609
Data supporting this are limited, and admission may best serve women with risk factors that portend early delivery .

337
00:41:42,609 --> 00:41:53,209
A few cases of antepartum fetoscopic surgery with vessel laser ablation are described (Hosseinzadeh, 2015; Johnston, 2014).

338
00:41:53,209 --> 00:41:57,822
However, current practice is early scheduled cesarean delivery.

339
00:41:57,822 --> 00:42:07,847
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021) recommends cesarean delivery at 34 to 36 weeks’ gestation.

340
00:42:07,847 --> 00:42:16,309
At delivery, the fetus is expeditiously delivered after the hysterotomy incision in case a vessel is lacerated during uterine entry.

341
00:42:16,309 --> 00:42:19,559
Delayed cord clamping is not encouraged.

342
00:42:19,559 --> 00:42:29,897
In all pregnancies, otherwise unexplained vaginal bleeding either antepartum or intrapartum should prompt consideration of vasa previa and a lacerated fetal vessel.

343
00:42:29,897 --> 00:42:35,834
In many cases, bleeding is rapidly fatal, and neonatal salvage is not possible.

344
00:42:35,834 --> 00:42:41,947
With less hemorrhage, however, it may be possible to distinguish fetal versus maternal bleeding.

345
00:42:41,947 --> 00:42:51,109
Various tests can be used, and each relies on the increased resistance of fetal hemoglobin to denaturing by alkaline or acid reagents .

346
00:42:51,109 --> 00:43:00,134
# Knots, Strictures, and Loops. Various mechanical abnormalities in the cord can impede blood flow and sometimes cause fetal harm.

347
00:43:00,134 --> 00:43:04,797
Of these, true knots are found in approximately 1 percent of births.

348
00:43:04,797 --> 00:43:15,697
These form from fetal movement, and associated risks include hydramnios and diabetes mellitus (Hershkovitz, 2001; Räisänen, 2013).

349
00:43:15,697 --> 00:43:23,059
Knots are especially common and dangerous in monoamnionic twins, which are discussed in Chapter 48 (p.

350
00:43:23,212 --> 00:43:34,462
845). In singleton fetuses, the stillbirth risk is increased four- to tenfold compared with those without knots (Airas, 2002; Sørnes, 2000).

351
00:43:34,412 --> 00:43:41,124
Knots can be found incidentally during antepartum sonography, and a “hanging noose” sign is suggestive .

352
00:43:41,124 --> 00:43:45,462
Threedimensional and color Doppler aid diagnostic accuracy .

353
00:43:45,462 --> 00:43:56,249
With these knots, optimal fetal surveillance is unclear but may include umbilical artery Doppler velocimetry, nonstress testing, or subjective fetal movement monitoring .

354
00:43:56,249 --> 00:44:04,299
Allowing vaginal delivery is suitable, and intrapartum fetal heart rate tracings do not differ from unaffected pregnancies .

355
00:44:04,299 --> 00:44:15,274
In these cases, cesarean delivery rates are not increased and cord blood acid-base values are usually normal (Airas, 2002; Maher, 1996).

356
00:44:15,274 --> 00:44:22,737
In contrast, false knots form from focal redundancy and folding of an umbilical cord vessel rather than knotting.

357
00:44:22,737 --> 00:44:25,562
These lack clinical significance.

358
00:44:25,562 --> 00:44:32,512
A cord stricture is a focal narrowing of the diameter that usually develops near the fetal cord insertion site .

359
00:44:32,512 --> 00:44:39,812
Pathological features typically include absent Wharton jelly at the narrowed segment and obliteration of cord vessels .

360
00:44:39,812 --> 00:44:43,349
In most instances, the fetus is stillborn .

361
00:44:43,349 --> 00:44:48,087
Even less common is a cord stricture caused by an amnionic band.

362
00:44:48,087 --> 00:44:54,549
Cord loops are frequently encountered and are caused by coiling around various fetal parts during movement.

363
00:44:54,549 --> 00:45:00,399
A cord around the neck—a nuchal cord —is common, and vaginal delivery is suitable.

364
00:45:00,399 --> 00:45:17,849
One loop is reported in 20 to 34 percent of deliveries; two loops in 2.5 to 5 percent; and three loops in 0.2 to 0.5 percent (Kan, 1957; Sørnes, 1995; Spellacy, 1966).

365
00:45:17,849 --> 00:45:29,449
During labor, up to 20 percent of fetuses with a nuchal cord have moderate to severe variable heart rate decelerations, and these are associated with a lower umbilical artery pH .

366
00:45:29,449 --> 00:45:33,299
Decelerations are not relieved by amnioinfusion .

367
00:45:33,299 --> 00:45:37,174
Cords wrapped around the body can have similar effects .

368
00:45:37,174 --> 00:45:47,924
Despite their frequency, nuchal cords are not associated with greater rates of adverse perinatal outcome (Henry, 2013; Masad, 2019).

369
00:45:47,924 --> 00:45:53,237
Last, in a funic presentation, the umbilical cord is the presenting part.

370
00:45:53,237 --> 00:45:58,374
These are uncommon and most often are associated with fetal malpresentation .

371
00:45:58,374 --> 00:46:04,999
A funic presentation in some cases is identified with placental sonography and color flow Doppler .

372
00:46:04,999 --> 00:46:08,974
Overt or occult cord prolapse can complicate labor.

373
00:46:08,974 --> 00:46:14,649
Thus, once identified at term, cesarean delivery is typically recommended.

374
00:46:14,649 --> 00:46:16,824
# Vascular.

375
00:46:16,824 --> 00:46:25,249
Cord hematomas are rare and generally follow rupture of an umbilical vessel, usually the vein, and hemorrhage into the Wharton jelly.

376
00:46:25,249 --> 00:46:35,637
Hematomas have been associated with abnormal cord length, umbilical vessel aneurysm, trauma, entanglement, umbilical vessel venipuncture, and funisitis .

377
00:46:35,637 --> 00:46:43,699
Most are identified postpartum, but hematomas are recognized sonographically as hypoechoic masses that lack blood flow .

378
00:46:43,699 --> 00:46:56,937
Sequelae include stillbirth or intrapartum abnormal fetal heart rate pattern (Abraham, 2015; Barbati, 2009; Sepulveda, 2005; Towers, 2009).

379
00:46:56,937 --> 00:47:01,087
However, case reports have described normal outcomes .

380
00:47:01,087 --> 00:47:07,562
Umbilical cord vessel thromboses are rare in utero events and seldom diagnosed antepartum.

381
00:47:07,562 --> 00:47:15,999
Approximately 70 percent are venous, 20 percent are venous and arterial, and 10 percent are arterial thromboses .

382
00:47:15,999 --> 00:47:29,024
These all have high associated rates of stillbirth, fetal-growth restriction, and intrapartum fetal distress (Minakami, 2001; Sato, 2006; Shilling, 2014).

383
00:47:29,024 --> 00:47:39,812
If these are identified antepartum as hypoechoic masses without blood flow, data from case reports support consideration of prompt delivery of viableaged fetuses .

384
00:47:39,812 --> 00:47:47,512
An umbilical vein varix can complicate either the intraamnionic or fetal intraabdominal portion of the umbilical vein.

385
00:47:47,512 --> 00:47:58,287
Sonographically and complemented by color Doppler, rare intraamnionic varices show cystic dilation of the umbilical vein that is contiguous with a normal-caliber portion.

386
00:47:58,287 --> 00:48:06,137
Of complications, an intraamnionic varix may compress an adjacent umbilical artery or can rupture or thrombose.

387
00:48:06,137 --> 00:48:13,562
A systematic review of 250 cases found that approximately one fifth is associated with other anomalies.

388
00:48:13,562 --> 00:48:17,399
Isolated cases had reassuring outcomes but

389
00:48:17,548 --> 00:48:23,073
typically required antenatal surveillance (di Pasquo, 2018).

390
00:48:23,023 --> 00:48:30,735
The rare umbilical artery aneurysm is caused by congenital thinning of the vessel wall with diminished support from Wharton jelly.

391
00:48:30,735 --> 00:48:37,198
Indeed, most form at or near the cord’s placental insertion site, where this support is absent.

392
00:48:37,198 --> 00:48:49,810
These are associated with SUA, trisomy 18, amnionic fluid volume extremes, fetal-growth restriction, and stillbirth (Hill, 2010; Vyas, 2016).

393
00:48:49,810 --> 00:48:57,210
At least theoretically, these aneurysms could cause fetal compromise and death by compression of the umbilical vein.

394
00:48:57,210 --> 00:49:05,935
With aneurysms measuring >5 cm, the blood reservoir within the aneurysm may pose a risk for high-output heart failure .

395
00:49:05,935 --> 00:49:11,285
These aneurysms may appear sonographically as a cyst with a hyperechoic rim.

396
00:49:11,285 --> 00:49:24,010
Within the aneurysm, color flow and spectral Doppler interrogation demonstrate either low-velocity or turbulent nonpulsatile flow (Olog, 2011; Shen, 2007b).

397
00:49:24,010 --> 00:49:33,135
Although not codified, management may include fetal karyotyping, antenatal fetal surveillance, and early delivery to prevent stillbirth .

398
00:49:33,135 --> 00:49:37,810
Some recommend delivery by cesarean to avoid aneurysm rupture.

